     1	"""Motion + push controllers.  Run on the central PC at ~20-50 Hz on the EKF pose estimate
     2	(`world.RoverEstimate`) and output unicycle (v mm/s, omega rad/s) -> `world.WheelCommand.from_unicycle`.
     3	
     4	Shared design notes (see NavParams below for the tuned numbers):
     5	
     6	Line tracking (`_Line`).  For a straight path from p0 to p1 with geometric direction `path_dir`, define
     7	    along, cross = to_local(pose)        # along-track / cross-track (left positive), via Pose.to_local
     8	    e_h = angle_diff(theta, reference_heading)     # reference_heading = path_dir, or path_dir+pi if reverse
     9	Exact unicycle kinematics give, regardless of forward/reverse:
    10	    d(along)/dt  = v_body * cos(phi),  d(cross)/dt = v_body * sin(phi)     (phi = theta - path_dir)
    11	and since reference_heading is path_dir shifted by a constant (0 or pi), e_h differs from phi by that
    12	same constant, so d(e_h)/dt = d(phi)/dt = omega always, and d(cross)/dt = v_body*sin(phi) = |v_body|*sin(e_h)
    13	in BOTH modes (forward: v_body=+|v|, phi=e_h; reverse: v_body=-|v|, phi=e_h+pi, sin flips, signs cancel).
    14	So a single law  omega = -k_h*e_h - k_y*e_y  (k_h,k_y > 0), with the *speed magnitude* |v| in the gain
    15	schedule, steers correctly for forward AND reverse without any special-casing -- this is also why a rover
    16	left of the line (e_y>0) must turn right (negative omega) whether driving forward or in reverse.
    17	
    18	Gain scheduling (`line_gains`).  Linearising sin(e_h)~=e_h gives the 2-state system
    19	    e_y' = |v| e_h,  e_h' = omega = -k_h e_h - k_y e_y
    20	whose characteristic polynomial is s^2 + k_h s + |v| k_y = 0, i.e. a mass-spring-damper with
    21	natural frequency wn = sqrt(|v| k_y) and damping 2 zeta wn = k_h.  Choosing a target (wn, zeta) at the
    22	operating speed gives  k_y = wn^2 / |v|,  k_h = 2 zeta wn.  zeta=1 (critical) is used everywhere here to
    23	get no-overshoot convergence in the ideal model; the derivative terms in PushController's law add extra
    24	damping against the un-modelled ~0.1 s motor lag and ~100-150 ms vision latency that the linear model
    25	ignores.
